---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const lang = 'en-us';
const post = {
  title: 'The Lemon Path: The Fragrant Walk Between Maiori and Minori',
  subtitle: 'Pergolas, Ancient Steps and IGP Sfusato Amalfitano Lemons',
  category: 'DISCOVERIES',
  date: 'July 15, 2026',
  readTime: '9 min',
  image: '/images/sentiero-dei-limoni-hero.webp',
  imageAlt: 'The Lemon Path between Maiori and Minori: pergola of Sfusato Amalfitano lemons on the Amalfi Coast',
  path: '/en-us/blog/lemon-path/',
};

const metaTitle = 'Lemon Path Maiori-Minori: Guide, Map and Tips 2026';
const metaDescription = 'Easy trek between Maiori, Minori and Ravello through Sfusato Amalfitano IGP lemon pergolas. Route, duration, difficulty, best season, and what to see along the path.';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/en-us/' },
  { name: 'Blog', url: '/en-us/blog/' },
  { name: 'The Lemon Path', url: '/en-us/blog/lemon-path/' },
];

const quickInfo = [
  { label: 'Most famous section', value: 'Maiori → Minori' },
  { label: 'Length', value: 'About 2 km' },
  { label: 'Duration', value: '45-60 minutes (one way)' },
  { label: 'Difficulty', value: 'T/E (Easy, steps)' },
  { label: 'Elevation', value: '~150 m (downhill Minori→Maiori uphill)' },
  { label: 'Best period', value: 'April - June (bloom) / September - October' },
  { label: 'Gear', value: 'Trekking shoes, water, hat' },
  { label: 'Access', value: 'Free, always open' },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How long is the Lemon Path?', a: 'The most famous section, between Maiori and Minori, is about 2 km and takes 45-60 minutes. An extension from Minori to Ravello via an ancient staircase doubles the length and takes 2-3 hours total. The route is entirely on ancient stone steps shaded by lemon pergolas.' },
  { q: 'Where does the Lemon Path start?', a: 'The two main starting points are: (1) from Maiori, the steps leading up from Via Capone near the Collegiata di Santa Maria a Mare; (2) from Minori, Via Casamare or the staircase climbing to the upper hamlets. The Minori → Maiori direction is downhill and easier; Maiori → Minori is uphill and more demanding.' },
  { q: 'Is it suitable for children?', a: 'Yes, it is one of the safest paths on the Amalfi Coast. It is almost entirely covered by lemon pergolas providing natural shade, the surface is stone but in good condition, and there are no exposed or dizzying sections. The only consideration is the steps: about 1,500-2,000 round trip. Suitable from age 6-7.' },
  { q: 'What are Sfusato Amalfitano lemons?', a: 'The Sfusato Amalfitano is the IGP lemon variety cultivated on Amalfi Coast terraces since the Middle Ages. Recognizable by its elongated "spindle" shape, thick fragrant skin, sweet juice with low acidity. It is the variety used for traditional limoncello, delizia al limone, and Amalfi Coast cuisine. Cultivation still requires manual work on terraces.' },
  { q: 'When is the best time to walk the Lemon Path?', a: 'Spring (April-June) and autumn (September-October) are ideal. In spring lemons bloom and the air is heavy with zagara (citrus blossom). In autumn fruits are ripe and visible on branches. Summer is not advised due to heat, though pergolas provide shade. Winter is always walkable on clear days, with crystal sea views.' },
  { q: 'Is there a map of the Lemon Path?', a: 'There is no official downloadable PDF. The route is fully mapped on OpenStreetMap, so it appears in Komoot, AllTrails, Organic Maps and most hiking apps: search for Sentiero dei Limoni and download the Maiori to Minori area offline before you start, because signal drops among the terraces. On the ground you follow wooden signs reading Sentiero dei Limoni plus painted marks at the junctions. The figures to keep in mind: about 2 km, 45-60 minutes, roughly 150 m of elevation.' },
  { q: 'How long is the Lemon Path from Minori to Maiori?', a: 'The same 2 km, but 35-50 minutes instead of 45-60, because from Minori the route is net downhill towards Maiori after a short initial climb. That is the direction to pick with children, in the heat, or late in the day. Walking Maiori to Minori you climb the steps first and finish downhill. Either way the return leg is 5 minutes by SITA bus or 20 minutes along the seafront.' },
  { q: 'Can you pick lemons from the path?', a: 'No, the groves are private property of local farmers. Touching or picking lemons is considered theft. However many points along the path have small farmer stalls selling fresh lemons, artisanal limoncello, lemon marmalade, and other products direct from producers. Prices are lower than in Amalfi tourist shops.' },
];

const sidebarLinks = [
  { label: 'Maiori', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/maiori/' },
  { label: 'Minori', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/minori/' },
  { label: 'Ravello', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/ravello/' },
  { label: 'Maiori vs Minori', href: '/en-us/blog/maiori-vs-minori/' },
  { label: 'SITA Bus', href: '/en-us/guide/sita-bus/' },
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Maiori vs Minori',
    category: 'GUIDES',
    date: 'June 23, 2026',
    image: '/images/maiori-vs-minori-hero.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Maiori and Minori: the two sister villages of the Coast',
    path: '/en-us/blog/maiori-vs-minori/'
  },
  {
    title: 'Ferriere Valley',
    category: 'DISCOVERIES',
    date: 'April 8, 2026',
    image: '/images/valle-ferriere-hero.jpg',
    imageAlt: 'Waterfall in Ferriere Valley, Amalfi - Amalfi Coast',
    path: '/en-us/blog/ferriere-valley/'
  }
];

const authorRole = 'Hiking & Heritage Editor · Amalfi Coast';
---

<BlogPostLayout
  lang={lang}
  post={post}
  dateModifiedISO="2026-08-10"
  metaTitle={metaTitle}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  quickInfo={quickInfo}
  faq={faq}
  sidebarLinks={sidebarLinks}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  relatedArticles={relatedArticles}
  pageKey="lemon-path"
>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <div>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-xl leading-relaxed mb-6 font-light">
      Few paths in the world reward effort with a fragrance that precedes every other sensation. The Lemon Path, connecting <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/maiori/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Maiori</a> to <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/minori/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Minori</a> in just over two kilometres, is one of them. Ancient stone steps shaded by Sfusato Amalfitano IGP lemon pergolas, sudden views over the sea and the upper hamlets of the Coast, and stops at family-run farms.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      It is an easy trek, accessible even for children, but with a density of landscape, history, and nature that surpasses many high-mountain walks. This is the complete guide: route, map, best period, what to see along the way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- The route -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">The Route: Maiori, Minori and Ravello</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The Lemon Path has two main variants. The best-known connects Maiori to Minori through the upper hamlets (about 2 km, 45-60 minutes). The longer one continues from Minori toward Ravello via a historic staircase (total 4-5 km, 2-3 hours).
    </p>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-[2rem] p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-med-gold font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Classic version</p>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-charcoal mb-2">Maiori → Minori (45-60 min)</h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">About 2 km, mostly uphill at the start from Maiori, then flat through the lemon groves, then downhill to Minori. Easiest and most iconic section. Suitable for everyone, including children.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-[2rem] p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-med-gold font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Extension</p>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-charcoal mb-2">Minori → Ravello (+1-1.5 h)</h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Continue from upper Minori for about 1,500 steps of a historic medieval staircase. Demanding climb compensated by arrival at Ravello's main square with its gardens.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-[2rem] p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-med-gold font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Reverse direction</p>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-charcoal mb-2">Minori → Maiori (more comfortable)</h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Same route reversed: from the Minori staircase a brief climb to the path, then mostly downhill to Maiori. Easier on average and less tiring. The views are the same in reverse order.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white rounded-[2rem] p-8 border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-med-gold font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Combined</p>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-charcoal mb-2">From Ravello to the Sea (downhill)</h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Start by SITA bus to Ravello, visit the gardens of Villa Rufolo and Villa Cimbrone, then walk down through the lemon groves to Minori. Total about 3 hours, one of the most spectacular on the Coast.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Map -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">The Lemon Path Map: How to Navigate It</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      You do not need a printed map, but knowing the waypoints in advance saves the two or three wrong turns most walkers make at the top of the Maiori staircase.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm mb-6">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Waypoints, Maiori to Minori</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-4 text-slate-600">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Start in Maiori.</strong> From the seafront, take the stepped alley climbing beside the Collegiata di Santa Maria a Mare. From the first landing the sea stays behind you the whole way.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-700"><strong>The climb.</strong> Roughly 15 minutes of steps up to terrace level. From this side it is the only real effort of the route.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-700"><strong>The pergola stretch.</strong> The flat central kilometre under the lemon canopies, with openings over the Minori valley. This is the section everybody photographs.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Descent into Minori.</strong> Steps down towards Via Casamare, arriving a few minutes from the Roman Villa Marittima and the seafront.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Signage.</strong> Wooden signs reading Sentiero dei Limoni plus painted marks at the junctions. There are few junctions, but the ones around the upper hamlets are where people go wrong.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      There is no official PDF map issued by the two municipalities: the signage on the ground is the reference. The route is fully mapped on OpenStreetMap, so it shows up in Komoot, AllTrails, Organic Maps and most hiking apps. Download the Maiori to Minori area offline before you set off, because signal drops among the terraces.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Sfusato lemons -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Sfusato Amalfitano: The King of Lemons</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The Sfusato Amalfitano IGP lemon is the variety that made the Amalfi Coast famous worldwide. Cultivated since the Middle Ages on steep terraces between Vietri sul Mare and Positano, it has unique characteristics.
    </p>
    <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm my-8">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Unique Characteristics</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Elongated "spindle" shape (hence the name "Sfusato"), thick rough skin, pale yellow flesh with few seeds, sweet juice with low acidity. The skin's fragrance is ten times more intense than industrial lemons. They keep for weeks without losing properties.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        IGP recognition obtained in 2001, now protecting the variety from imitations. On the Lemon Path pergolas, fruits often weigh over 250 grams and reach 15 centimetres in length.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      Along the path you pass dozens of family farms, many of which sell directly to the public: fresh lemons, lemon marmalade, artisanal limoncello, delizia al limone. Buying here means supporting the farmer terraces that have defined the Coast's landscape for a thousand years.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- What to see -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">What to See Along the Path</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The Lemon Path is not only nature. Along the route you find ancient churches, panoramic viewpoints, and corners of rural archaeology.
    </p>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm flex gap-5">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-med-gold/10 flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center text-med-gold font-bold text-xs sm:text-sm">1</div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Church of San Pietro Apostolo (Torre)</h4>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Small church in the upper hamlet of Maiori, dated 13th century. Panoramic view over the Gulf of Salerno and cultivated terraces. Open only during Sunday services.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm flex gap-5">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-med-gold/10 flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center text-med-gold font-bold text-xs sm:text-sm">2</div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Twin-Village Belvedere</h4>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">About halfway, an opening in the pergolas offers one of the most photographed views on the Coast: Maiori on the right with its one-kilometre beach, Minori on the left with its small bay, the blue sea at the centre.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm flex gap-5">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-med-gold/10 flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center text-med-gold font-bold text-xs sm:text-sm">3</div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Family-run farms</h4>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Three or four farms along the route open their stalls to walkers. You can often taste limoncello and marmalade before buying. Prices typically half those of the tourist centre.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm flex gap-5">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-med-gold/10 flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center text-med-gold font-bold text-xs sm:text-sm">4</div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Basilica of Santa Trofimena (Minori)</h4>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">At arrival in Minori, the 12th-century Basilica preserves the relics of the town's patron. Worth a 15-minute visit to see the crypt and majolica floors.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Local Tips -->
  <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-slate-100">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-6">
      <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-med-gold flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 22h4"/><path d="M15.09 14c.18-.98.65-1.74 1.41-2.5A4.65 4.65 0 0 0 18 8 6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 1 .23 2.23 1.5 3.5A4.61 4.61 0 0 1 8.91 14"/></svg>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">5 Tips for the Lemon Path</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">1. Go early morning in spring</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-xs sm:text-sm">From April to June lemons are in bloom and the air smells of zagara. Early morning (by 9 am) the pergolas are cool and almost empty. Recommended breakfast in Maiori before starting.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">2. Buy directly from farmers</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-xs sm:text-sm">Small farmer stalls along the path offer limoncello, marmalade, and lemons at half the price of tourist shops. It also means supporting the farmer terraces.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">3. Combine with Minori beach</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-xs sm:text-sm">After the path, Minori beach is 5 minutes on foot from the Basilica. Swim and lunch of lemon pasta in one of the restaurants on the seafront.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">4. SITA bus to return</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-xs sm:text-sm">From Minori, <a href="/en-us/guide/sita-bus/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">SITA</a> buses return to Maiori in 5 minutes. Frequency every 15-30 minutes. Avoid walking the reverse climb.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">5. Extension to Ravello</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-xs sm:text-sm">If you are fit, from Minori climb to Ravello via the historic medieval staircase. Three hours total, but reaching Villa Rufolo after walking through lemon groves is an experience that justifies the entire Coast trip.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</BlogPostLayout>
